The task in Week 3 focuses on applying a supervised machine learning algorithm called K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) to predict whether water is drinkable or not. The dataset used is stored in a CSV file named w1.csv. The first step is loading the data using Python’s csv library. Since not all the columns are relevant for classification, certain columns are removed to clean the dataset. After that, the features (input variables such as measurements of water quality) are separated from the labels (the final classification: drinkable or not drinkable).

To train and evaluate the model properly, the dataset is divided into two parts: training data (80%) and testing data (20%). This ensures that the model is tested on unseen examples, making the results more reliable. Two versions of the KNN algorithm are created, one using 5 neighbors and the other using 21 neighbors. Both models are trained on the same training set and then tested on the testing set.

The performance of each model is measured using accuracy, which checks how many predictions were correct. The results are printed for both k=5 and k=21. Based on the accuracy, the better model is chosen automatically. A new sample data point with multiple numeric features is then tested using the better-performing KNN model to determine whether the water sample should be classified as drinkable or not.

This experiment highlights the importance of choosing the right value of “k” in KNN. A small k may be too sensitive to noise, while a very large k may oversimplify the prediction. By testing both values, the code demonstrates how to find a balance and select the best model for the data. In short, this week’s work introduces practical use of KNN for classification, feature selection by removing unnecessary columns, and the evaluation of model performance on real-world data.
